"Expresses a business decision as input conditions and output rules under an explicit matching policy.\n\nUse when: Specify repeatable eligibility, pricing or routing rules. Find rule gaps, overlaps and conflicting outputs systematically.\n\nAvoid when: The main need is explaining policy dependencies or sequential participant behavior.\n\nVisual grammar: Input columns contain typed conditions; output columns contain results. Each row denotes one rule, with a declared hit policy. FEEL expressions define intervals, comparisons and literal values precisely.\n\nBuild: Declare the decision's inputs, types and allowed outputs. Choose the hit policy before entering representative rules. Test boundaries, missing inputs and overlapping matches against realistic cases.\n\nCheck: Conditions cover intended valid cases without unintended gaps. Multiple matches behave correctly under the declared hit policy. Output values and interval boundaries agree with the policy text.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming first-match behavior without declaring it. Using ambiguous natural-language thresholds such as around ten.\n\nExample: A shipping table combines destination and parcel weight to return a service class, with explicit weight boundaries and a unique-match policy.\n\nAlternatives: Decision requirements diagram, Decision tree."
